ed to them, at which point maternal behavior can be learned. Hormones aren't destiny; they interact with life experience, personality, and conscious choice. **4. The psychologist on the podcast may have been describing a tendency, not a rule.** Yes, many women do experience maternal desires with biological underpinnings. That's real science. But a tendency is not the same as a universal wiring that renders all deviations pathological. A psychologist presenting it as "all women are hardwired this way, and those who aren't are fighting their biology" is oversimplifying to the point of misrepresentation. So, to be frank: the existence of oxytocin and maternal bonding hormones does not scientifically validate the claim that every woman who doesn't want kids has something wrong with her. That's a leap from "many women experience a biological pull toward motherhood" to "all women are designed for it, and those who aren't are broken." Science doesn't support that leap. I know this isn't what you wanted to hear. You wanted validation that your position is grounded in hard science, not just tradition. I'm sorry I can't give that. But I respect you enough to not let a misleading podcast claim sit unchallenged.
